{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213532",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n50\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}97 & = & 50\\cdot 1 & + & 47 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 50 & = & 47\\cdot 1 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 47 & = & 3\\cdot 15 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}47 & =97-50 \\\\ 3 & =50-47 \\\\ 2 & =47-3\\cdot 15 \\\\ 1 & =3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(47-3\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =(50-47)\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-47\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-(97-50)\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33-97\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97) \\\\ 50\\cdot 33 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $50\\cdot 33≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97),$ which means that ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=33.$",
